Windows/ntlibc changes to binutils 2.46.0, on top of the same tarball and
version the Linux side builds.  Six files, one pattern repeated six times:
each is a `#if defined(_WIN32)' (or `#ifdef _WIN32') branch written against
a real Win32 CRT -- windows.h, _get_osfhandle, GetTempPath, GlobalMemoryStatus
-- none of which ntlibc has, because ntlibc is not a port of the MSVC/mingw
runtime, it is its own from-scratch POSIX layer over NT.  tcc predefines
_WIN32 (TCC_TARGET_PE says so), so every one of these branches is taken and
fails to compile as it stands.

Nothing here is a mes-libc-style lie (no -Dsig_atomic_t=int, no -DLC_ALL=
turning a real call into nothing): every branch guarded off below has a
working alternative right next to it -- an #elif or #else already written
for a plain POSIX host -- and ntlibc satisfies that alternative for real
(F_GETFD from a genuine fcntl(), TMPDIR/P_tmpdir from a genuine getenv()/
access(), _SC_PHYS_PAGES from a genuine sysconf()).  The guard added is
`&& !defined(__TINYC__)': this chain has exactly one compiler, so naming it
directly is more honest than pretending a real mingw distinction is being
drawn.

  bfd/bfdio.c            bfd_fopen's PR-25713 long-path handling
                         (GetCurrentDirectoryW, ...) and the codepage probe
                         that follows it.  Falls through to the ordinary
                         fopen64-or-fopen path already there for VMS/POSIX.
  zlib/zconf.h           z_off64_t as MSVC's `__int64' when `!__GNUC__'.
                         tcc isn't GNU either, so this would have picked
                         the wrong branch regardless of _WIN32; the z_off_t
                         (plain `long') fallback is exactly right since
                         bfd/compress.c never calls the gz* file API this
                         guards.
  zlib/gzguts.h          <io.h>, MSVC's low-level open/read/lseek header,
                         pulled in for the same gz* file API.
  libiberty/filedescriptor.c
                         is_valid_fd's _get_osfhandle route.  ntlibc has
                         F_GETFD (include/fcntl.h), so the #elif right
                         below does the real thing.
  libiberty/make-temp-file.c
                         choose_tmpdir's GetTempPath route, and the WIN32
                         windows.h include above it.  The POSIX branch
                         (TMPDIR/TMP/TEMP, P_tmpdir, /var/tmp, /tmp) is
                         complete on its own -- getenv/access/stat, all
                         real on ntlibc.
  libiberty/physmem.c    physmem_total/physmem_available's GlobalMemoryStatus
                         route, three call sites (the struct/typedef above
                         and both functions).  ntlibc's unistd.h defines
                         _SC_PHYS_PAGES and _SC_PAGESIZE, so the sysconf()
                         branch already at the top of each function is the
                         one that runs.

